      "notes": "All three claims are supported by their quoted evidence. E2 is a factual historical record appropriately coded measurable. E17 quotes the MAC's own language and is coded measurable \u2014 this is a borderline call (MAC finding vs. projection) but the MAC statement is a published institutional finding, not a forecast, so measurable is defensible. E4 is coded projected and the quote is from a gov.uk-backed source summarising expected effect, which is appropriate. Tier integrity is clean. The 70% figure cited in the rationale (from E28) is not a formal claim but is used in the rationale; it is sourced. Direction 'improves' is well-supported: removing an income-based barrier to family reunification is a paradigm O10 improvement. Magnitude 'moderate' with the caveat about unspecified target level is appropriately hedged. No party-identity language or motive attribution detected. No false balance issue \u2014 the verdict does not reach mixed/uncertain. No lever leakage \u2014 the verdict judges the liberty effect on O10, not immigration policy as such. Minor observation: E14 (reunitefamiliesuk.co.uk, advocacy) is not cited in the claims, so advocacy-source concern does not arise. Overall the verdict is well-constructed and passes."
